00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:20.150
So what is the secret behind reverse psychology?

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.690
It's all because of a thing called "Reactance".

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:29.440
Reactance is the concept that we are all afraid
to lose our freedom and we will do whatever

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.740
it takes to hang onto it.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.830
If our freedom is threatened, for example
if someone takes away our ability to make

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.870
decisions we react out of fear.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:44.290
If someone tells us we can't have fast food
anymore, we instantly crave fast food like

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:48.780
never before, because we are reacting out
of fear of loosing our freedom.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.160
Reverse psychology works so well because when
you threaten to take away somebody's freedom

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.639
their natural reaction is to go out of their
way to exercise that freedom and prove to

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:01.600
you that you're unable to take it away.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.979
Just take a look at fair Romeo and Juliet.

00:07:04.979 --> 00:07:08.280
They first met and liked each other, sure.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:15.009
But it was only when both their families forbade
them from being together that they fell crazily

00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:17.490
in love and ended up dying for one-another.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:22.990
It's one of the most powerful examples of
reverse psychology in literature.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:31.020
But the greatest example of reverse psychology
of all time comes from ancient Chinese history.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:37.710
In the greatest book on war ever written,
The Art of War, Sun Tzu wrote: "Appear weak

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:42.260
when you are strong, and strong when you are
weak".

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:47.380
During the period of the Three Kingdoms in
China, around 200 AD - the country's multiple

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:53.729
leaders were at war and one such leader Zhuge
Liang was known as one of the greatest Chinese

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:56.130
strategists of all time.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:01.600
During the war of the three kingdoms, Zhuge
Liang was held up in a fort with only a handful

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.889
of soldiers.

00:08:02.889 --> 00:08:09.070
When his enemy Sima Yi learnt of his location
and heard he had so few men defending his

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.070
fort.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:15.410
Sima Yi set off with an army of thousands
of men, to conquer Zhuge Liang and take over

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:22.660
his fort but Liang heard about Yi's campaign
before he arrived.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:27.610
Instead of bolting all the doors shut and
putting what few soldiers he had along the

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:35.779
battlements with bows and arrows to defend
the fort, then hiding away somewhere, he came

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:42.329
up with a plan involving a magnificent feat
of reverse psychology.

00:08:42.329 --> 00:08:47.920
Liang ordered all the doors and gates of the
fort to be opened up wide.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.740
He ordered his few soldiers to dress up as
civilians and to sweep the grounds inside

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.259
the fort and the roads outside with brooms.

00:08:58.259 --> 00:09:04.660
Liang then sat calmly atop of the fort, looking
out at the approaching army and said not a

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:12.029
word but melodically played his Guqin - an
ancient Chinese instrument that is plucked.

00:09:12.029 --> 00:09:18.699
As Yi arrived with his huge army, weapons
in hand, he was greeted with open gates, peasants

00:09:18.699 --> 00:09:28.540
cleaning the streets and a calm, fearless
Liang sat atop his empty fort, still playing

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:32.019
his instrument.

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:38.279
Sensing suspicion Yi instantly thought it
was a trap and that Liang had setup an ambush

00:09:38.279 --> 00:09:44.689
involving hiding his soldiers in the surrounding
hills to circle Yi's army from behind.

00:09:44.689 --> 00:09:50.939
Instead of rushing the empty fort, Yi quickly
ordered his men to retreat with great haste,

00:09:50.939 --> 00:09:56.829
fearing an imminent ambush, an ambush which
of course didn't exist.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:10:04.290
And so, using reverse psychology, Liang had
quelled an otherwise deadly attack.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:10.410
Liang's incredible strategy has since come
to be known as "Empty Fort Strategy" and it

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:17.910
made its way into the famous Chinese Thirty
Six Stratagems as the 32nd stratagem.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:23.889
From Chinese masters of war to modern day
advertising, the power of reverse psychology

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:25.610
is timeless.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:30.420
Like this advert for the British Army, that
uses reverse psychology to almost makes you

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:34.040
feel guilty for not joining the army.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:42.709
And my personal favourite, this advert...
for a university degree in Reverse Psychology.

00:10:42.709 --> 00:10:51.470
An Amsterdam hotel has perfected the art of
reverse psychology, by branding itself "The

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.769
worst hotel in the world".

00:10:53.769 --> 00:11:00.301
Hans Brinker Budget Hotel is smack bang in
the centre of Amsterdam and is, by their own

00:11:00.301 --> 00:11:08.709
accord very cheap, smelly, cold, not too clean,
has absolutely no luxuries and is generally

00:11:08.709 --> 00:11:11.890
a damn awful place to stay.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:16.999
Online reviews for the hotel include "A bus
shelter offers the same facilities."

00:11:16.999 --> 00:11:18.929
and "What is that smell?

00:11:18.929 --> 00:11:21.350
I demand to know what that smell is."

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:26.640
But instead of trying to improve their standards,
they just made these advertising campaigns...

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:34.779
and this... and this...

00:11:34.779 --> 00:11:38.059
And, well would you believe it, it worked.

00:11:38.059 --> 00:11:42.980
Since releasing these brutally honest ads
the hotel's business is booming.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:51.730
Hans Brinker's 511 beds in 127 rooms are nearly
fully occupied all year round.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:55.310
And yes, it's still awful.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:12:01.559
Reverse psychology has certainly worked out
favourably for Hans Brinker but not so much

00:12:01.559 --> 00:12:05.000
for another advertiser.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.259
Smoking kills, we all know it, even smokers.

00:12:08.259 --> 00:12:13.649
But addiction is a difficult and emotionally-driven
problem to overcome.

00:12:13.649 --> 00:12:19.899
In early 2000, following a treaty signed at
the 2003 World Health Assembly in Geneva,

00:12:19.899 --> 00:12:26.949
it became the law in most countries that cigarette
and tobacco packaging had to contain a health

00:12:26.949 --> 00:12:27.949
warning.

00:12:27.949 --> 00:12:33.549
Some countries adopted written warnings and
others contained highly graphic pictorial

00:12:33.549 --> 00:12:34.709
warnings.

00:12:34.709 --> 00:12:40.279
In the following years many world governments
put funds into anti smoking ads on national

00:12:40.279 --> 00:12:41.279
television.

00:12:41.279 --> 00:12:46.899
Such ads are usually highly graphic and usually
end in the protagonist either developing cancer

00:12:46.899 --> 00:12:49.010
or erectile dysfunction.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:53.759
The World Health Organisation were pretty
pleased with themselves and believed this

00:12:53.759 --> 00:12:59.629
visceral new advertising would vastly reduce
the number of smokers worldwide by using fear

00:12:59.629 --> 00:13:01.589
to make them quit.

00:13:01.589 --> 00:13:06.639
And for a while the world ignorantly believed
that the strategy was working.

00:13:06.639 --> 00:13:09.089
But what have we learnt?

00:13:09.089 --> 00:13:14.980
That the more times you tell somebody they
can't or shouldn't do something the more likely

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.239
they are to do it.

00:13:17.239 --> 00:13:23.259
And sure enough, multiple recent studies have
shown that warning labels on smoking products

00:13:23.259 --> 00:13:30.139
has absolutely no effect on smoking or quitting
rates whatsoever.

00:13:30.139 --> 00:13:36.480
Moreover a recent study by experts at Tel
Aviv university and New York university concluded

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:43.129
that, bizarrely, warning labels on cigarettes,
no matter how horrific, actually increase

00:13:43.129 --> 00:13:45.420
cigarette sales.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:49.429
Researchers showed various packets of cigarettes
to test participants, some had warning labels

00:13:49.429 --> 00:13:55.220
on, such as "Smoking Kills" and "Smoking Causes
Cancer" etc. and some of the packets had no

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.199
warnings on them at all.

00:13:57.199 --> 00:14:02.910
The participants were asked which packets
they would rather purchase and the vast majority

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.170
of them chose the packets with warning labels.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:12.040
There's something interesting going on here,
this has a lot to do with reverse psychology

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.319
but there's a bit more to it.

00:14:15.319 --> 00:14:20.619
The researchers found out that the test participants
believed that the brands that put warning

00:14:20.619 --> 00:14:27.420
labels on their cigarette packages were perceived
as more honest and trustworthy and so in some

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:34.209
strange way, the perceived quality and value
of that brand's cigarettes went up, in the

00:14:34.209 --> 00:14:38.910
minds of the participants.

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:44.939
Reverse psychology isn't just a mental quirk,
it's a tool that many successful people have

00:14:44.939 --> 00:14:48.420
used, among them Freddie Mercury.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:55.429
After Queen recorded Bohemian Rhapsody, a
six-minute long song, every record company

00:14:55.429 --> 00:15:02.480
they spoke to said it was too long to be successful
and would never be played on the radio.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:09.269
So Freddie Mercury gave a copy of the song
to his friend and radio DJ, Kenny Everett,

00:15:09.269 --> 00:15:11.740
as a gift.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:16.050
Freddie told him never to to play it on air.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:23.049
Of course Everett did the complete opposite
and played the song on air over 14 times in

00:15:23.049 --> 00:15:25.399
a single radio slot.

00:15:25.399 --> 00:15:31.790
It was soon number one in the UK charts and
it went on to grace the number one spot again

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.759
in 1991 after Mercury's death.

